>
  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    Conversion Collective is a Los Angeles based consultancy working on sales
    systems and revenue for B2B companies. The client is my sister. The site needed
    to read as premium and credible to founders and revenue leaders rather than as
    a generic consultant template, and it needed to drive one specific action:
    booking a free sales audit.
  </p>

  <h2>The approach</h2>
  <p>
    A dark editorial single-page site — the visual direction we settled on was
    "McKinsey meets Vogue" — built around a single conversion path: hero, the
    problem she solves, proof through stats and client logos and testimonials, the
    offer, then booking. The actual call-to-action lives on its own
    <code>lets-work.html</code> page so the booking flow isn't competing with the
    rest of the argument.
  </p>

  <h2>The decision worth explaining</h2>
  <p>
    Every editable piece of copy — navigation, stats, client logos, services,
    process steps, the founding-client offer, testimonials, FAQ — lives in a single
    <code>siteData</code> object in <code>script.js</code>. Updating a price or
    swapping a testimonial is editing a JavaScript object, not hunting through
    markup.
  </p>
  <p>
    The exception is deliberate: hero copy, the problem statement and the about
    text stay as prose directly in <code>index.html</code>, because that content
    benefits from being read and edited in context rather than abstracted into
    data. The rule I applied was roughly — if it's a list of things, it's data; if
    it's an argument, it's prose.
  </p>

  <h2>Technical notes</h2>
  <p>
    No framework and no build step. Fluid type through <code>clamp()</code>,
    mobile-first CSS, and dark and light section theming driven by custom
    properties rather than separate stylesheets.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google Analytics, Yandex Metrica and Search Console are wired in from the
    start. The site's job is to convert and be measurable, not only to look good —
    and a conversion-focused page with no instrumentation is an assertion rather
    than a result.
  </p>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    Design and full build, end to end — content structure, visual direction and
    implementation.
  </p>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>Live at convertcollective.com.</p>
</Work>
